The Retroactive History of TlebiAmeriki – Expanded (The Rise of the Stacy)
When President Bronny James declared the Sovereign Free State of TlebiAmeriki in 2041, the nation was pure will and sharp jawline. What it still lacked was grace.
That changed the night Jayda Wayda walked into the temporary capital in downtown Los Angeles.
The Arrival of the Stacy
She did not arrive as a politician. She arrived as inevitability.
Jayda Wayda—already a myth in the old United States for her unapologetic glamour, diamond standards, and refusal to ever look average—had watched the secession from afar with quiet interest. When the borders closed, she simply boarded a private jet, landed at the newly renamed Tlebi International, and requested an audience with the President.
No one knows exactly what was said in that first closed-door meeting. Witnesses only report that when the doors opened two hours later, Bronny James looked different. Not softer. Sharper. As if the final piece of the national aesthetic had clicked into place.
Within weeks she was named First Consort of TlebiAmeriki—a title invented for her, because “First Lady” felt too small.
The Soft Power Doctrine
Where President James built the hard architecture of the new nation (borders, energy, physical standards), Jayda Wayda built its aura.
She instituted the National Presentation Code:
	•	Public appearances required intentional beauty.
	•	Mediocrity in dress or grooming was quietly discouraged through social pressure rather than law.
	•	The phrase “Look like you belong here” became a national slogan.
Under her influence, TlebiAmeriki’s cultural exports shifted. Hollywood did not die—it evolved. The old franchise machines were replaced by high-glamour, high-confidence cinema that made the rest of the continent feel underdressed just by watching. Fashion houses that once dictated trends from New York and Paris found themselves chasing looks that originated in the hills above Malibu.
She also created the Stacy Accords—an informal but strictly observed social hierarchy that rewarded excellence in presentation, loyalty, and feminine power. Women who embodied the standard rose quickly. Those who rejected it were free to leave… and many did.
The Partnership
The public image of the First Couple became legend.
Bronny: silent, imposing, the living Gigachad. Jayda: radiant, untouchable, the living Stacy.
Together they projected a single message to the old United States and the world:
We did not secede to escape you. We seceded because we outgrew you.
State dinners became events of almost religious intensity. Foreign dignitaries left TlebiAmeriki both impressed and vaguely insecure. One European ambassador famously wrote in his private journal: “I have never felt so underdressed in my own skin.”
The Later Years
When President James completed his third term in 2053, he did not fade into retirement. He and Jayda Wayda withdrew to a cliffside compound overlooking the Pacific—the same cliffs that held the giant Gigachad statue.
From there they continued to shape the nation’s culture in quieter ways. Their children (the exact number remains a matter of polite speculation) were raised under the dual doctrine of physical excellence and aesthetic supremacy. By the 2060s, the next generation of TlebiAmeriki leadership all carried the unmistakable stamp of both parents: the unblinking confidence of the Chad and the effortless, devastating glamour of the Stacy.
Historians would later write that the secession succeeded not only because of strength, but because strength finally had a face beautiful enough to make the rest of the world want to look away… and keep looking anyway.
The final official portrait of the era shows them side by side: Him in black, jaw set. Her in diamonds, expression calm and absolute.
Beneath it, the simple caption that defined a nation:
He left. She made leaving look like winning.
